   "id": "time-to-their-date",
   "label": "How long until their date",
   "used_by": [
    "deadline-they-gave"
   ],
   "over": [
    "conversation-history"
   ],
   "over_status": "decided",
   "defined": true,
   "counts": "The working time between today and each date the buyer stated that is still ahead, nearest first, with how much of the run-up has gone.",
   "needs": [
    "gmail#email-message",
    "gmail#email-thread",
    "slack#slack-message",
    "meet#transcript"
   ],
   "breaks": "A date that has already passed drops out of the forward half, which it probably should not, and that is a read nobody has written.",
   "title": "How long until their date",
   "intro": "",
   "sections": {
    "What it counts": {
     "html": "<p>The working time between today and each date the buyer stated that is still ahead, nearest first. Working time, the way every other gap in the library is counted. Against it, the time between today and the day they said it, which is how much of the run-up has already gone. Two numbers rather than one, because \"six months left\" means something different when they told you last week and when they told you in July. Which records it needs and when the number stops meaning anything are not written in the count's fields yet, like every other count.</p>\n<p>It counts within <a href=\"../assemblies/conversation-history.md\"><code>conversation-history</code></a>, decided when the count was written rather than derived, because a date the buyer gave you is said on a thread and nowhere else.</p>"
    },
    "What it cannot see": {
     "html": "<p>Whether the date is still the date. A committee moves, a budget year does not, and nothing on the record says which kind this one is; the read grades on that and the count does not know. It also cannot see a date that has already passed quietly, which drops out of the count the day it does and should probably not: a deadline the buyer gave and you both sailed past is worth knowing about, and that is a read nobody has written.</p>"
    }
   },
   "sectionOrder": [
    "What it counts",
    "What it cannot see"
   ],
   "file": "counts/time-to-their-date.md",
   "markdown": "---\nid: time-to-their-date\nlabel: \"How long until their date\"\nused_by: [deadline-they-gave]\nover: [conversation-history]\nover_status: decided   # 21 September: confirmed when the arithmetic was written, which is what made it answerable\ndefined: true\ncounts: The working time between today and each date the buyer stated that is still ahead, nearest first, with how much of the run-up has gone.\nneeds: [gmail#email-message, gmail#email-thread, slack#slack-message, sms#text-message, meet#transcript]\nbreaks: A date that has already passed drops out of the forward half, which it probably should not, and that is a read nobody has written.\n---\n\n# How long until their date\n## What it counts\n\nThe working time between today and each date the buyer stated that is still ahead, nearest first.\nWorking time, the way every other gap in the library is counted. Against it, the time between today\nand the day they said it, which is how much of the run-up has already gone. Two numbers rather than\none, because \"six months left\" means something different when they told you last week and when they\ntold you in July. Which records it needs and when the number stops meaning anything are not written\nin the count's fields yet, like every other count.\n\nIt counts within [`conversation-history`](../assemblies/conversation-history.md), decided when the\ncount was written rather than derived, because a date the buyer gave you is said on a thread and\nnowhere else.\n\n## What it cannot see\n\nWhether the date is still the date. A committee moves, a budget year does not, and nothing on the\nrecord says which kind this one is; the read grades on that and the count does not know. It also\ncannot see a date that has already passed quietly, which drops out of the count the day it does and\nshould probably not: a deadline the buyer gave and you both sailed past is worth knowing about, and\nthat is a read nobody has written.\n",
   "silent": false
