   "file": "scenarios/bops/cards/ask-buying-process.md",
   "markdown": "---\nid: ask-buying-process\norder: 23\nkind: ask\nsignal: steps-on-their-side\nsignal_status: provisional   # mapped by reading the card, not yet confirmed\ncounts: [steps-the-buy-needs, steps-with-a-sign]\ncounts_status: provisional   # which numbers this quotes, not yet confirmed\nabout: [you]\narrives: 2026-09-17\ndocuments: []\nreply:\n  module: field\n  placeholder: \"The steps, in the order they happen. A line each is plenty.\"\nactions:\n  - label: \"Tell Me\"\n    style: filled\n    confirms: field\n    says: \"Thank you. I'll hold every live council against that list and tell you which steps I have seen no sign of.\"\n  - label: \"Not Now\"\n    says: \"Okay. I'll ask again when a council is close enough for it to matter.\"\nphone:\n  act: \"Answer\"\n  act_does: open\n  said: \"Thank you. I'll hold every council against it.\"\n  done: \"Thank you\"\n  done_text: \"I've written it down as what a buy at a council takes. I'll hold every live one against it and tell you which steps I have seen no sign of. I'll only ever say I have seen no sign, never that they have not done it.\"\n---\n\n# What has to happen inside a council before anyone can sign?\n\nI can see everything that passes between you and them. I can see nothing that happens in the room after you leave.\n\n## Known so far\n\n- The steps I have caught the shadow of, because somebody mentioned them: Holbrook's procurement window, which closes on 16 October. A committee at Felton that Stephen Walsh referred to in August. Nothing else.\n- Two of them, out of a list I do not have. I do not know whether that is two of four or two of nine.\n\n## What's missing\n\n- The list itself. You have sold into councils for years and you know what it takes: a case for the spend, whatever assessment their rules require, a committee that has to sit, a signature. I have never been told any of it.\n- What each one looks like when it has happened. A committee date is something they tell you. A business case is something you might never see. I need to know which is which, or I will treat your silence about a thing as their inaction on it.\n\n## What your answer changes\n\n- I hold every live council against your list and tell you which steps I have seen no sign of, and which of those you said you could help with.\n- I can also tell you the shape of a sale: Westmoor ticking steps off without you means a process is running, and Felton talking for months with nothing started means they are still deciding what they want. Those are worked differently and today I cannot tell them apart.\n- If you tell me you can supply the figures a case needs, that becomes an offer I can draft rather than a thing you remember to mention.\n\n## Where your answer is saved\n\n- As \"what a buy at a council takes\", against the kind of buyer rather than against one council. Editable, dated, and shown to you before it is ever used.\n- I will never say a council has not done a step. I will say I have seen no sign of it and ask you. Work done in their building does not reach me, and the two look identical from here.\n",
